{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 28, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Oregon is set to end its experiment with decriminalizing drugs, with a new law taking effect that makes possessing small amounts of hard drugs a crime again. The law, HB4002, gives those caught with illicit drugs the choice to be charged with possession or undergo treatment, which includes behavioral health programs and a \"deflection program\" to avoid fines.\n\nUnder the new law, possessing small amounts of hard drugs for personal use will be a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail. The law also introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near public areas like parks. However, it encourages counties to create treatment alternatives to divert people from the criminal justice system and towards addiction and mental health services.\n\nThe law's backers say it allows counties to develop programs based on their resources, while opponents argue it may create a confusing and inequitable system. The Portland police chief, Bob Day, explained that officers will call a deflection dispatch line to determine eligibility and connect people to a mobile behavioral health outreach team.\n\nIf the team cannot make contact within 30 minutes, officers will take the person into custody. Day emphasized that officers cannot wait around for the team to arrive. By the age of 18, people in this country should know how to eat healthily and avoid substance abuse. A person who is struggling with addiction may need help from a therapist, but they should not have to go shopping for a solution. The law aims to tackle the complex issue of addiction, but it may be a challenge to implement effectively.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.35134006}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.77221096}, {"token": " set", "logprob": -0.7552321}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -1.8119648e-0